    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The development of new resist materials is vital to fabrication techniques for next- generation microelectronics. Hybrid organic-inorganic materials are a promising class of resists because they have higher etch resistance, are more impervious to pattern collapse, and are more absorbing of extreme ultraviolet (EUV) radiation than organic resists. However, there is limited understanding about how they behave under irradiation. In this work, we study the molecular layer deposition of Hf- and Al-based hybrid thin film resists, known as “hafnicone,” “oxygen- rich alucone,” and “standard alucone.” These resists were grown at 100ºC using the metal precursor tetrakis(dimethylamido)hafnium(IV) or trimethylaluminum as well as the organic precursor ethylene glycol. Electron-beam lithography, scanning electron microscopy and profilometry are used to investigate the resist performance of hafnicone, oxygen-rich alucone, and standard alucone. Like hafnicone, oxygen-rich alucone behaves as a negative tone resist that can resolve 50-nm line widths, though the line patterns of oxygen-rich alucone are more sharply defined than those of hafnicone. Whereas hafnicone displays a sensitivity of 400 μC/cm2 using 3 M HCl as the developer, the sensitivity of oxygen-rich alucone—4800 μC/cm2 using 0.125 M HCl—is not as good. Compared to oxygen-rich alucone, standard alucone offers superior (sub-25 nm) resolution at the expense of lower sensitivity (>13,000 μC/cm2 using 0.01 M HCl). In addition to determining the resist properties of the three films, we characterize hafnicone via x- ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and infrared spectroscopy (IR) to investigate its patterning mechanism, which is described in the context of classical nucleation theory. This study of hafnicone and alucone hybrid MLD demonstrates the ability for the bottom-up vapor deposition of inorganic resists to be used in advanced e-beam and EUV lithographic techniques.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
